{"summary":"Most amendments identify real inconsistencies in the carbonate contract between solver outputs, validation, and persistence. The remaining proposals add useful observability and regression coverage. I would accept all six; the migration cleanup is lower severity than the chemistry-correctness fixes, but still technically sound.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-2-46f297bc","decision":"accept","rationale":"`fallback_all_co2` reports a pH derived from carbonic-acid dissociation but hardcodes all DIC as `CO2(aq)` with zero bicarbonate/carbonate. That makes the returned state internally inconsistent and underreports `bicarbonate_mg_total`, which then skews derived TDS/conductivity in that regime. Reprojecting species at the reported fallback pH is the coherent fix."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-26ba2577","decision":"accept","rationale":"The documented carbonate contract says fallback paths should emit a structured diagnostic, but the current fallback helpers return silently. Because those paths materially alter chemistry behavior and otherwise leave no audit trail, adding debug-level structured logging is a valid robustness and operability improvement."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-234f259a","decision":"accept","rationale":"There is preset-layer coverage for `predicted_carbonate_ph`, but no integration test that the runtime materialization path (`WaterState::from_source_profile_for_volume_l` plus the core solver) preserves distinct shipped-profile pH outcomes. Since preset validation and runtime resolution are implemented in separate codepaths, an integration assertion on ordering and spread is justified and would catch regressions those existing tests would miss."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-88ff4519","decision":"accept","rationale":"`migrate_v2_to_v3` rescales `bicarbonate_mg_total`, but `normalize_loaded_carbonate_state` immediately recomputes that field from DIC/alkalinity after deserialization. That makes the migration rescale behaviorally dead in the load path and leaves misleading code for future migration authors. Removing it and documenting bicarbonate as solver-derived is a maintainability win with no intended state change."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-21162875","decision":"accept","rationale":"The solver returns `NEUTRAL_FALLBACK` at exact zero DIC, while arbitrarily small positive DIC under the same alkalinity can produce the alkaline fallback ceiling. Because several live update paths clamp DIC to exactly zero before resolving, that discontinuity is reachable in normal simulation and can cause a one-tick pH snap. The zero-DIC branch should be made limit-consistent with neighboring states."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-102f3c3c","decision":"accept","rationale":"Source-water validation in `tank_data` and runtime resolution in `tank_core` currently encode different carbonate acceptance rules. That means the same chemistry payload can be rejected as a TOML preset yet accepted from a save/runtime catalog and then simulated, which is an avoidable contract split. Unifying both paths behind one helper or one explicit shared rule is the correct maintainability and correctness direction."}]}